    <help format="markdown"><![CDATA[
        @salmonhelp@

        **Salmon quant**

        Salmon quant can be run in two modes:

        - **Reads**: salmon maps the reads itself. Selective alignment is the
          default mapping mode in salmon 2.x; the new sketch option switches
          to pseudoalignment, which is faster and uses less memory.
        - **Alignment**: salmon quantifies from an existing alignment BAM.
          Salmon 2.x requires the alignments to be *read-name-grouped*, so
          the tool runs `samtools collate` on the input BAM before
          quantifying.

        **Input**

        The reference transcriptome can be selected from a *pre-built index*
        (if your Galaxy instance provides one) or provided as a FASTA file in
        the history, in which case the index is built on the fly. Optionally a
        reference genome FASTA can be supplied to build a decoy-aware index.

        Reads may be plain FASTA/FASTQ, or gzip or bzip2 compressed; salmon
        2.x detects the compression automatically.

        **Output**

        - `quant.sf`: the transcript-level quantification table with columns
          Name, Length, EffectiveLength, TPM and NumReads.
        - `quant.genes.sf`: gene-level quantification, only produced when a
          gene map (GTF/GFF or transcript-to-gene TSV) is provided. Use the
          *ignore transcript versions* option when the transcript FASTA
          carries version suffixes that the gene map lacks.
        - Optionally the computed alignments (returned as a sorted BAM) and
          an auxiliary files tarball for MultiQC (`aux_info/`, `libParams/`,
          `lib_format_counts.json`).
    ]]></help>
